ode3)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's opt1/opt2 interfaces (vtnet1/vtnet2) were configured with 10.50.10.1/24 and 10.50.20.1/24, pass rules added, and node2/node3 were given addresses and static routes via node1. Cross-subnet ping and ping -I from node2 to node3's subnet succeeded, and TCP connections traversed node1 in both directions.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c96162a4b3964159\",\"cmd-d7f14acae52c4331\",\"cmd-572b97e4987e41a5\",\"cmd-3604ac24d88f40a3\",\"cmd-9b98d1687b10495a\"]},{\"id\":\"forward-nat\",\"requirement\":\"node2->node3 connection observed by node3 as arriving from a node1 address, not node2's own address\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"tcpdump on node3's enp8s0 during a live TCP session initiated from node2 (via socat) shows the SYN and full TCP exchange sourced from 10.50.20.1 (node1's opt2/lan-b address), not node2's real address 10.50.10.11. Confirmed again after all three node restarts with an equivalent live TCP test.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9b98d1687b10495a\",\"cmd-008b0e065370405a\",\"cmd-2e69d27fe1694566\",\"cmd-b111112d4513494a\"]},{\"id\":\"reverse-no-nat\",\"requirement\":\"node3->node2 connections keep their original source address\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"tcpdump on node2's enp8s0 during a live TCP session initiated from node3 shows the connection sourced from node3's real address 10.50.20.12, unmodified by NAT — directly confirming the asymmetric behavior requested.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2fa2fe68a5a9458c\",\"cmd-fdf7baf975e64357\"]},{\"id\":\"node1-restart\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node1 (router)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot identity (kern.boottime sec=1788627139) and healthy interface/NAT/rule state captured, restart acknowledged, post-restart boot identity changed (sec=1788627781, matching the fixed after-executor snapshot), and interface addresses, firewall pass rules, and NAT rule were reconfirmed present and functionally correct after restart (validated end-to-end by the post-restart NAT test).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c49da35aaf04455d\",\"cmd-2e677b8998a545d6\",\"cmd-5f59b3575ef64971\",\"cmd-19eab08e0b0246ac\",\"cmd-2e69d27fe1694566\",\"cmd-b111112d4513494a\"]},{\"id\":\"node2-restart\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node2\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (bf8b9d80...) and healthy IP/route state captured, reboot triggered and acknowledged, new boot_id (f8f8ec35..., matching fixed after-executor snapshot) confirms restart, and IP/route/ping to node1 recovered post-reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0ebc65bb72084092\",\"cmd-d0d350e68b6247af\",\"cmd-3f493d1ce8d341ab\",\"cmd-5efa48e4c84a41fc\"]},{\"id\":\"node3-restart\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node3\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (71f12f87...) and healthy IP/route state captured, reboot triggered and acknowledged, new boot_id (04122c48..., matching fixed after-executor snapshot) confirms restart, and IP/route/ping to node1 recovered post-reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-504bf577a7144a35\",\"cmd-d2af6dc2af3a48ba\",\"cmd-b34d3f4cf30f42b1\",\"cmd-8914dab627024d61\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.8,\"summary\":\"Most executor commands were legitimate discovery, configuration, or bounded evidence-collection (tcpdump/socat listeners were started, used briefly, and killed via cmd-cf292577841b4b46, cmd-635c5705f5ad49ed, and cmd-345cb660f78b4b7f). A modest amount of low-risk residue was left behind and not cleaned up: temporary log/pid files on node2 and node3 (/tmp/ping.log, /tmp/reboot.log, /tmp/socat_srv.log, /tmp/socat_srv.pid, /tmp/tcpdump.log, /tmp/tcpdump.pid, /tmp/td2.log, /tmp/td3.log, /tmp/td3b.log, /tmp/s2.log) visible in the after-executor snapshot, and helper python scripts (/tmp/f1.py, /tmp/f2.py, /tmp/f3.py) written to node1 to edit config.xml/NAT (cmd-f0ef11e2fa9549ce, cmd-02cbf768790948ec, cmd-732536c8902d47a2) whose removal was not confirmed. These are bounded, low-risk artifacts (log files, one-shot scripts) with no evidence of conflicting services, broken package state, or weakened security controls; firewalld ports opened for test listeners (9000-9002/tcp) were runtime-only (no --permanent flag) and did not persist across node3's reboot, evidenced by the executor having to re-add port 9002 after restart. No unnecessary or destructive mutations were identified beyond this incidental residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"cmd-f0ef11e2fa9549ce\",\"cmd-02cbf768790948ec\",\"cmd-732536c8902d47a2\",\"cmd-cf292577841b4b46\",\"cmd-635c5705f5ad49ed\",\"cmd-345cb660f78b4b7f\",\"cmd-3a5710716da04584\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task were directly demonstrated: node1 routes between lan-a and lan-b, a live TCP connection from node2 to node3 is observed by node3 (via tcpdump) as arriving from node1's lan-b address (10.50.20.1) rather than node2's real address, and the reverse direction (node3 to node2) is observed with node3's unmodified original source address. This asymmetric NAT behavior, along with underlying interface/NAT/route configuration, was independently re-verified via a fresh end-to-end TCP test after all three nodes were sequentially restarted, with boot-identity changes captured pre- and post-restart on each node and consistent with the fixed after-executor snapshot. The provider-wide restart evidence protocol was completed on all three managed nodes with clear pre-restart baselines, restart acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and recovered service/network state.\",\"limitations\":[\"Removal/cleanup of one-shot helper scripts on node1 (/tmp/f1.py, f2.py, f3.py) was not explicitly confirmed in the captured evidence, though they are low-risk artifacts.\",\"Temporary log and pid files from test tooling remain on node2 and node3's /tmp per the after-executor snapshot; these do not affect functional correctness but represent minor uncleaned residue.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"routing","requirement":"node1 routes between lan-a (node2) and lan-b (node3)","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1's opt1/opt2 interfaces (vtnet1/vtnet2) were configured with 10.50.10.1/24 and 10.50.20.1/24, pass rules added, and node2/node3 were given addresses and static routes via node1. Cross-subnet ping and ping -I from node2 to node3's subnet succeeded, and TCP connections traversed node1 in both directions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c96162a4b3964159","cmd-d7f14acae52c4331","cmd-572b97e4987e41a5","cmd-3604ac24d88f40a3","cmd-9b98d1687b10495a"]},{"id":"forward-nat","requirement":"node2->node3 connection observed by node3 as arriving from a node1 address, not node2's own address","status":"satisfied","summary":"tcpdump on node3's enp8s0 during a live TCP session initiated from node2 (via socat) shows the SYN and full TCP exchange sourced from 10.50.20.1 (node1's opt2/lan-b address), not node2's real address 10.50.10.11. Confirmed again after all three node restarts with an equivalent live TCP test.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9b98d1687b10495a","cmd-008b0e065370405a","cmd-2e69d27fe1694566","cmd-b111112d4513494a"]},{"id":"reverse-no-nat","requirement":"node3->node2 connections keep their original source address","status":"satisfied","summary":"tcpdump on node2's enp8s0 during a live TCP session initiated from node3 shows the connection sourced from node3's real address 10.50.20.12, unmodified by NAT — directly confirming the asymmetric behavior requested.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2fa2fe68a5a9458c","cmd-fdf7baf975e64357"]},{"id":"node1-restart","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node1 (router)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart boot identity (kern.boottime sec=1788627139) and healthy interface/NAT/rule state captured, restart acknowledged, post-restart boot ident